# This repository is hash-anchored, so the one thing git must never do is change a byte on the way
# in or out of a checkout. Line-ending translation does exactly that, silently and per-platform:
# with core.autocrlf=true a Windows clone rewrites every text file, which changes its sha256 and
# puts a stray \r on the end of every filename inside a checksum manifest. The symptom is
# bewildering rather than obvious -- `sha256sum -c` reports
#
#     sha256sum: 'some/file.py'$'\r': No such file or directory
#
# for a file plainly sitting right there.
#
# Hence: translate nothing. The repo's bytes are the repo's bytes.
* -text

# NOTE, deliberately different from the `genesis` repo. That one additionally pins
#
#     *.py text eol=lf
#
# because its Python blobs are stored LF. The blobs in THIS repository are stored CRLF, so adding
# that line here would re-normalise every Python file on the next checkout -- rewriting bytes,
# changing every sha256, and invalidating any published checksum or signed release that covers
# them. Do not copy that line in without first converting the blobs deliberately, in its own
# commit, and re-issuing whatever checksums depend on them.

# Belt and braces for formats that must never be touched even if the default above is relaxed.
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary
*.rar    binary
*.dat    binary
*.asc    binary
*.sig    binary
*.parquet binary

# Shell scripts are pinned to LF. Measured 7 Aug 2026: the committed blobs for scripts/*.sh
# contain CR bytes, while local clones here run with core.autocrlf=true, which masks that in the
# working tree. A clone made with the usual Linux/macOS default (autocrlf=false) would therefore
# receive the CR bytes verbatim, and a CRLF shell script does not merely hash differently -- it
# fails to run, with bash reporting only "unexpected end of file". Pinning eol=lf removes the
# ambiguity so the scripts are the same bytes, and runnable, on every platform.
# No published manifest in this repository covers these files, so pinning them changes nothing
# that was ever signed or checksummed.
*.sh text eol=lf
